[web-to-mobile]
## From Web Platform to Mobile Learning

//problem: The original concept replicated a feature-heavy web platform and was not suited to fast, on-the-go learning.
//research: We reviewed the previous experiment, analyzed the market, and explored how users consumed educational content on mobile devices.
//iterations: We shifted to a story-based format with full-screen media, concise lesson pages, and quick access to active courses.
//outcome: A focused mobile learning product designed around short, engaging content rather than desktop workflows.

//gallery: mobile-lesson.png, mobile-lessons.png, mobile-story.png


[removing-editor]
## Removing the Mobile Editor

//problem: The team assumed that creators needed a complete lesson editor inside the app, making the product increasingly complex.
//research: We tested multiple editor concepts, conducted 20+ interviews, and surveyed more than 500 early users.
//iterations: The research showed that the primary audience was experienced experts working with content teams, not first-time solo creators. We removed the editor and simplified lesson setup.
//outcome: A leaner product aligned with real creator workflows, without investing further in a feature the core audience did not need.

//gallery: editor-settings.png, editor-cards.png, editor-access.png


[social-content]
## Social-First Content System

//problem: Creators needed to reuse existing content without rebuilding every lesson specifically for the platform.
//research: We analyzed different content formats and established creator workflows.
//iterations: We added reusable content packs, consistent media formats, automated content tagging, and guidelines for both video and text-based lessons.
//outcome: A flexible publishing system that made existing content easier to adapt, distribute, and recommend inside the app.

